# SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for the custom-login-slug rewrite rules.
#
# These land in two places that run at different times, in different shells,
# on different machines:
#
#   lib/03-dynamic-configs.sh  -> wp-security.conf   (Proxmox host, bash)
#   payload/stages/04-...      -> .htaccess          (inside the VM, sh)
#
# They cannot share a function. They CAN share this file, and they now do.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# The two used to be maintained separately and drifted: one sent the bare slug
# to /wp-login.php, the other to /wp-admin/index.php. Whichever ruleset won, a
# request to /<slug> reached wp-admin unauthenticated, WordPress redirected to
# the login page, the login-slug mu-plugin rewrote that back to /<slug>, and
# the browser looped until it gave up with "The page isn't redirecting
# properly". Nothing failed, nothing logged an error, and validate-wordpress
# reported the slug serving the login page — which was true, and was the first
# hop of the loop.
#
# @@SLUG@@ is replaced with the configured slug by whichever consumer reads it.
# Keep every line a complete RewriteRule; the consumers indent but do not parse.
RewriteRule ^@@SLUG@@/?$ /wp-login.php [L,QSA,E=WPVM_SLUG:1]
RewriteRule ^@@SLUG@@/(.+)$ /wp-admin/$1 [L,QSA,E=WPVM_SLUG:1]
